  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 AM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nAn ongoing complex of stratiform rain with embedded thunder over \nSEMO will continue to decay through early this morning to start the \nweekend. By this afternoon, a H5 shortwave associated with a broad \nupper level trough may initiate additional showers and \nthunderstorms. Coverage will be fairy isolated and confined to \nmainly western Kentucky, but did bump up NBM PoPs to a broad 20-30% \nchance as the 0z CAMs are hinting at disorganized convection \ndeveloping during peak heating. While most locations will remain \ndry, a few spots could pick up a quarter to half inch with localized \nheavy downpours. Combined with an increase in cloud cover today, \npeak heat index values now look to remain near 100 degrees. Skies \nquickly clear out tonight with model soundings supporting the \npotential for some patchy ground fog with a shallow inversion \ndeveloping overnight.\n\nAs a broad H5 ridge of high pressure builds east on Sunday, drier \nconditions combined with abundant sunshine and southerly return flow \nwill yield more hot and humid conditions. Heat index values by the \nNBM are now progged to rise between 100-105 degrees Sunday through \nWednesday, which would meet the 4 consecutive day criteria (100\ndegrees) for a Heat Advisory. However, daily shower and \nthunderstorm chances quickly return by Wednesday which creates \nsome uncertainty as the other scenario would focus on the 1 day\ncriteria (105 degrees) for Monday and Tuesday. Regardless, heat\nheadlines will likely be needed in the near future. Daily rain \nchances continue through the end of the week as a cold front \neventually provides relief with high temperatures trending out \nof the lower 90s into the upper 80s by Friday. Some of the ML \nguidance is hinting at some severe weather potential Wednesday \ninto Thursday, but it is highly contingent on the timing of the \nfront with the better kinematics progged north of the FA at this\ntime.",
